{"anchor": {"begin": [1, 0, 525.33], "end": [1, 0, 721.33], "env": "theorem", "file": null, "head": null, "id": 1, "kind": "approximate", "label": null, "labels": [], "line": null, "number": "1.1", "source_sha256": "6f08fdd78557147c229793275a956ed29698e2e0b965b4e3d5677f392eb49218", "statement": "Theorem 1.1. For every ν > 0 there exist a force f ∈ C ∞ c ( R3 × (0, ∞ ); R3 ), a compact set K ⊂ R3 , and smooth velocity and pressure fields u, p on R3 × [0, 1) satisfying ∂ t u + (u · ∇)u − ν ∆ u + ∇p = f, ∇ · u = 0, u(·, 0) = 0, (1.1) such that supp u(·, t) ∪ supp p(·, t) ⊂ K for every 0 ≤ t < 1, sup 0≤t<1 ∥u(t)∥ L2( R 3) < ∞ , lim sup t↑1 ∥u(t)∥ L ∞ ( R 3) = ∞ . Consequently, there is no smooth solution (u, P) on R3 × [ 0, ∞ ) with the same force and initial datum whose kinetic energy is uniformly bounded sup t≥0 1 2 ∫︁ R3 |u(x, t)|2 dx < ∞ . This establishes alternative (C) in the Millennium problem statement for Navier–Stokes as stated by Fefferman in [13]. Compact support also yields the corresponding construction on T3 = R3 / Z3 , establishing alternative (D) in [13]; see Corollary 10.6. 1", "title": null}, "blockers": [], "class": "proved", "clauses": [], "correspondence": {"by": "Christian Garry (drafted with Claude Code; second review by ChatGPT, checked)", "confidence": "high", "coverage": "full", "note": "theorem_1_1 carries the whole statement for every ν > 0: the force, compact set and fields with all listed properties, and no global smooth solution with uniformly bounded kinetic energy for the same force and zero datum (lim sup ‖u(t)‖_L∞ = ∞ is stated as unbounded speed near t = 1). The project's Comparator declaration navier_stokes_breakdown_R3 carries only the non-existence consequence, in Fefferman's alternative (C) form: some datum and a force in the decay class admit no smooth solution of bounded energy; it does not state the blowup construction or the compact support of the force.", "source": "confirmed"}, "decls": {"NavierStokes.Comparator.ForceCondi